#!/bin/sh
# Refuse a commit the guards do not pass.
#
# ## Why this exists
#
# "Any failure means no commit" is the project's rule and it was held by whoever
# remembered to run the guards first. Three of the guards in tools/check.py were written
# and then never registered there, so a convention this project holds to was checked only
# by whoever thought of it -- which is the state conventions.md calls a wish rather than a
# convention. The same is true one level up: a rule about what must happen before a commit
# belongs in the thing that makes the commit.
#
# ## What it runs, and what it does not
#
# `tools.check --fast`, not the full run. The full run takes about fourteen minutes here,
# 800 seconds of it the test suite, and a hook that costs fourteen minutes is a hook people
# disable. --fast skips the test suite, the doc examples and nothing else; the full run is
# still required before pushing, and the CI workflow does it on every push.
#
# It checks the **working tree**, not the staged set, because that is what tools.check
# reads. So it will pass a commit whose staged subset is broken while the tree as a whole
# is fine. Said here rather than left to be discovered: this hook answers "is the tree
# green", and nothing automatic in this repository answers "is the staged subset coherent"
# -- that is what reading `git diff --cached --name-only` is for.
#
# ## Enabling it
#
# Git does not run hooks from a tracked directory on its own. Once, per clone:
#
#     git config core.hooksPath tools/githooks
#
# That is deliberately a person's decision rather than something a script does on their
# behalf: it changes where git looks for every hook in the repository.
#
# ## Skipping it
#
# `git commit --no-verify` skips it, as with any pre-commit hook. That is a feature, not a
# hole -- the alternative is a hook people turn off permanently the first time it is wrong.
# A skipped guard is still a red guard, and CI will say so on the push.

set -eu

# A Python with the dependencies. The .venv in this repository has no pandas, so the
# interpreter on PATH is not a safe default.
#   1. BACKTEST_PYTHON, for a machine where neither of the next two is right
#   2. the project's own, which CLAUDE.md names
#   3. python3, so a fresh clone on another machine gets a useful error from the guards
#      rather than "no such file" from the hook
if [ -n "${BACKTEST_PYTHON:-}" ] && [ -x "${BACKTEST_PYTHON}" ]; then
    PY="${BACKTEST_PYTHON}"
elif [ -x /opt/anaconda3/bin/python ]; then
    PY=/opt/anaconda3/bin/python
elif command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    PY="$(command -v python3)"
else
    echo "pre-commit: found no Python to run the guards with." >&2
    echo "  Set BACKTEST_PYTHON to an interpreter that has pandas, or install the" >&2
    echo "  dependencies: pip install -e \".[dev]\"" >&2
    exit 1
fi

ROOT="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
cd "$ROOT"

echo "pre-commit: ${PY} -m tools.check --fast"
if "${PY}" -m tools.check --fast; then
    exit 0
fi

cat >&2 <<'MESSAGE'

pre-commit: refused. A guard did not pass, and any failure means no commit.

Run the one that failed on its own for its full output, for example:

    /opt/anaconda3/bin/python tools/check_language.py

If a guard really could be ignored, the guard itself should change -- see
conventions.md, "The fixed check". Weakening it to get a commit through is the one
thing this project refuses.

To commit anyway: git commit --no-verify. The guard stays red, and CI will say so.
MESSAGE
exit 1
